-- title:
--   The unramified cyclotomic order and its localizations
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a formal proof that a real circulant Hadamard matrix of positive order $n$ exists only for $n=1$ and $n=4$. Such a matrix has entries $\pm1$, satisfies $HH^{\mathsf T}=nI$, and has the form $H_{ij}=h(j-i)$ with indices in $\mathbb Z/n$. Its first row becomes an element $f$ of the integer group ring $\mathbb Z[\mathbb Z/n]$ with $f f^{*}=n$, where $f^{*}$ reverses the indices. Two-adic descent reduces every order $n>1$ to $n=4u^2$ with $u$ odd; for $u>1$, evaluation at characters with values in cyclotomic rings and a local argument at a prime above $2$ give a contradiction.
--
--   1. **Cyclotomic order.** For $\eta\in\mathbb C$, the subring $\mathbb Z[\eta]\subseteq\mathbb C$ with its generator $\eta$.
--   2. **Localization.** For a prime ideal $\mathfrak m$ of $\mathbb Z[\eta]$, the localization $\mathbb Z[\eta]_{\mathfrak m}$, which has characteristic zero.
--   3. **Power basis.** If $\eta$ is a primitive $L$th root of unity with $L>0$, the integral power basis of $\mathbb Z[\eta]$ over $\mathbb Z$ generated by $\eta$.
--
--   At a maximal ideal over a prime $p\nmid L$, the localization has maximal ideal generated by $p$:
--   $$
--   \mathfrak m\,\mathbb Z[\eta]_{\mathfrak m}=(p),
--   $$
--   which is proved by differentiating $\eta^L=1$.
